  "content": {
    "id": "v2-frequency-practice-slot-13-lower-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "A fair coin is tossed 200 times and lands on heads 112 times.\nExplain why the relative frequency of heads is not equal to the theoretical probability.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "The relative frequency is $0.56$ but the theoretical probability is $0.5$. Random variation means 200 tosses need not give exactly 100 heads.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}rf&=\\frac{\\text{Frequency of heads}}{\\text{Total trials}}\\\\rf&=\\frac{112}{200}\\\\rf&=0.56\\end{align*}$$A fair coin has $P(\\text{heads})=\\frac12=0.5$. Random variation means 200 tosses need not give exactly 100 heads, so the two values do not match.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 45
  },
